{"availability":"available","full":"caseywebdev/react-list","name":"react-list","description":":scroll: A versatile infinite scroll React component.","category":"Developer tools","language":"JavaScript","stars":1970,"forks":173,"created":"2014-06-23T20:29:38Z","updated":"2026-07-02T04:39:42Z","license":"MIT","branch":"master","homepage":"https://caseywebdev.github.io/react-list","topics":[],"demo":"https://caseywebdev.github.io/react-list","color":"#323057","ink":"#d6caff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-17T19:51:43.023Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-17T19:51:43.023Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-17T19:51:43.023Z","lastDemoCheckedAt":"2026-10-11T19:47:19.588Z","discoveredVia":[{"kind":"curated-list","name":"Awesome React Components","url":"https://github.com/brillout/awesome-react-components"}],"metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-11T19:47:19.588Z","stars":1968,"forks":174},{"at":"2026-10-17T19:51:43.023Z","stars":1970,"forks":173}],"technologies":["React"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["A versatile infinite scroll [React] component.","This happens when specifying the uniform type without actually providing uniform size elements. The component attempts to draw only the minimum necessary elements at one time and that minimum element calculation is based off the first element in the list. When the first element does not match the other elements, the calculation will be wrong and the component will never be able to fully resolve the ideal necessary elements.","The calculations to figure out element positioning and size get significantly more complicated with margins, so they are not supported. Use a transparent border or padding or an element with nested elements to achieve the desired spacing."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/caseywebdev/react-list/blob/master/README.md","fingerprint":"5ebd967d031a600600f6f2d5978d0937c0e5ab6058bc3e105504cb9e427e96d2","extractedAt":"2026-10-11T19:47:19.588Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-11T19:47:19.588Z","projectInsight